tools + `gmeow candidate` CLI sharing one core. All six issue acceptance criteria
(AC1-AC6) execute on the production surface; `make check` green.

- SCOPED-DOWN ENHANCEMENT (weaker mechanism, forward path): the plan's Enhancement-Audit
  item "packet-closure-as-KB composition" (submit_candidate reuses an AuthoringPacket's
  depth-1 CBD + definitional closure as the candidate KB) is NOT delivered as specified.
  Implementation reality: the bundle's graph/authoring-briefs carries the SPARSE packet
  index (covered-term IRIs + grounding cells + margins), NOT the per-term axiom/neighbour
  closure — that closure lives only in the base graph / a repo checkout (assemble_packet).
  So a bundle-served closure-as-KB is not reconstructable checkout-free. WEAKER MECHANISM
  SHIPPED: submit_candidate records the target packet/slice as PROVENANCE
  (gmeow:candidateForPacket / candidateForSlice) and requires the KB supplied explicitly;
  the covered-term IRIs the packet lists resolve to full content via lookup_term/doc_card.
  FORWARD PATH: a dev-mode (McpMode::Dev, repo-anchored) tool could assemble the closure
  turtle from a checkout and feed it as the candidate KB, closing the loop for in-repo
  authoring without changing the shipped consumer surface.

- BY-DESIGN BOUNDARY (not a descope): slice_brief serves the packet as canonical turtle +
  a JSON reading of the bundle's sparse packet body (the user-selected scope). It does NOT
  reconstruct the full AuthoringPacket model's render_text, because the model's rich
  per-term fields (coat/axioms/neighbours/closure) are dropped in to_turtle and are not in
  the bundle graph. This matches the shipped bundle's contents; the rich content is reached
  per covered-term IRI via the existing lookup_term / doc_card tools.

## 2026-07-17 — #1459 native authoring gates, stage-2 gap remediation (branch paudley/1459-native-authoring-gates)

- RESIDUAL WEAKER PROXY (faithful parity, no regression): the R3e docs-example term gate
  (`authoring_integrity::docs_undeclared_findings`) checks a doc `gmeow:` reference against a
  GLOBAL allowlist — every term declared ANYWHERE in the ontology/DSL/generated corpus. So it
  catches a pure typo (a term that appears nowhere) but NOT a plausible-but-wrong term that
  happens to collide with a real term declared in some unrelated slice. This is a strictly
  weaker check than the other five authoring families (which are exact set/closure/ownership
  invariants). It faithfully reproduces the retired `_docs_allowlist` Python gate — there is NO
  regression versus the deleted gate, and the completion-adversary cleared it as an
  enhancement-decline (the issue asked for parity with the retired cluster, not a stronger
  check). Recorded here as an honest below-maximal item. FORWARD PATH: scope the allowlist
  per-document — restrict each doc example to the terms that doc actually declares/imports
  rather than the global union — so a wrong-but-real term is caught. Not done in this branch
  (it is a net-new capability beyond the retired gate's contract, not a fix to a regression).

- The other five gap-remediation items in this stage were FIXED, not deferred, so they are NOT
  recorded here: the unreadable-directory silent-degradation hole (walkers now hard-fail;
  non-vacuity floor added to the live aggregator), the DARK ownership + example-coverage folds
