  (`canonical_abstract_is_standardized`) compares them WHITESPACE-NORMALIZED (`norm_ws`:
  collapse whitespace runs to single spaces + trim), not byte-for-byte. The user explicitly
  signed off on this relaxation at planning: the retired Python test used `_norm_ws`, and the
  CITATION.cff `abstract` is a YAML folded block (`>-`) whose line wrapping folds to spaces, so
  byte-identity is only meaningful post-parse and normalized equality is the faithful
  recreation. This is strictly weaker than literal byte-identity (it cannot catch a pure
  whitespace/wrapping divergence that a downstream byte consumer might care about). FORWARD
  PATH: if a single canonical source is ever established (Principle 4 — one authored abstract,
  the other two generated projections), the parity gate becomes moot and byte-identity is
  guaranteed by construction; until then the normalized check is the correct guard for three
  independently-authored surfaces.

